Start by rewriting the given system of equations in exponential form.

Given:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log _{2}(\sin x \cos y) & =-\frac{3}{2} \\
\log _{2}\left(\frac{\sin x}{\cos y}\right) & =\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

Rewriting these equations:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sin x \cos y & = 2^{-\frac{3}{2}} \\
\frac{\sin x}{\cos y} & = 2^{\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Simplify the exponential expressions.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sin x \cos y & = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4} \\
\frac{\sin x}{\cos y} & = \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

